  {
   "name": "Code width",
   "expr": "1 LSB = VREF / 65536",
   "where": {
    "VREF": "reference voltage (VCC for the LTC1865 in SO-8)"
   },
   "note": "Code 0 is at VIN = 0; code 65535 begins at VIN = VREF – 1LSB, with VIN = IN+ – IN–.",
   "page": 9
  },
  {
   "name": "Total cycle time",
   "expr": "tCYC = 16 • SCK + tCONV",
   "where": {
    "SCK": "one shift clock period",
    "tCONV": "conversion time, 3.2µs max"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 4
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "LTC1864 conversion cycle",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "A rising edge on CONV starts a conversion, which takes tCONV. If CONV stays high after that, the part goes to sleep and draws only leakage. When CONV falls the part returns to sample mode and SDO leaves Hi-Z. Each bit, B15 first, leaves SDO on a falling SCK edge; the host latches it on the rising edge. Clocks beyond the sixteenth with CONV still low shift out zeros with no end."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Automatic power-down",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Between conversions the converter powers itself down, so supply current falls with sample rate: 850µA typical at 250ksps and about 2µA at 1ksps."
  },
  {
   "topic": "LTC1864 analog input",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The input is differential and unipolar: the result is IN+ minus IN–, zero code at zero difference and full scale at VREF minus 1LSB. Both inputs are sampled at one instant, so noise common to both is rejected. Grounding IN– and joining VREF to VCC gives a rail-to-rail span on IN+."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reference input",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The VREF voltage sets full scale. The LTC1864, and the LTC1865 in MSOP, work with references from 1V up to VCC. In the LTC1865 SO-8 the reference is joined to VCC inside, so the span equals VCC. A span as small as 1V lets many sources connect without a gain stage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "LTC1865 conversion cycle and channel select",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The cycle is the same as the LTC1864's. After CONV falls, the first two rising SCK edges latch a 2-bit word from SDI (SGL/DIFF, then ODD/SIGN) that sets the multiplexer for the next conversion; further SDI bits are ignored until the next cycle. Data in and data out move at the same time (full duplex), shifted on falling SCK and captured on rising SCK. The multiplexer channel-selection table gives the four input pairings; in single-ended mode the selected channel is measured against GND."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Grounding",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Use an analog ground plane grounded at a single point, built on a printed circuit board rather than wire wrap. Run the grounds (AGND and DGND on the LTC1865 MSOP, GND otherwise) to the analog plane by the shortest leads."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bypassing",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "VCC and VREF must be clean: a change on either during a conversion shows up as error or noise in the code. Bypass each with at least 1µF tantalum to the analog plane, on the shortest leads."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Driving the analog inputs",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The switched-capacitor inputs draw brief current spikes. These settle quickly with a source resistance below 200Ω or with a fast op amp such as the LT1211, LT1469, LT1807, LT1810, LT1630, LT1226 or LT1215. With higher source resistance or a slow-settling amplifier, the spikes must have died away before the conversion starts."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Evaluation board layout",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The LTC1864 evaluation board keeps the analog traces wider and almost all of them on the top side, uses a ground layer split into analog and digital grounds, and a supply layer that carries the 5V digital supply with the analog ground repeated."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sending samples over telephone wire",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "An LTC1864 can report a remote sensor over cheap four-conductor telephone cable. A Manchester encoder XORs clock with data; D flip-flops retime the two first so their edges line up and the XOR makes no glitches. The coded stream averages 50% duty cycle, so it can be transformer coupled, and an LTC1485 RS485 transceiver drives the line. Each frame is 31 zeros, a start bit and the 16 data bits, one sample every 48 clocks at 1MHz from an LTC1799 oscillator. At least 18 zeros before the start bit let the receiver find the start again after losing sync, and the serial-to-parallel stage needs those 18 zeros so data bits cannot trigger it. The receiver, suited to an FPGA, runs at about 8 times the transmit clock and tolerates clock error well; it outputs data and a strobe for each valid bit, which shift registers turn back into a parallel word. With 5V AC-family CMOS the receive clock tops out at 20MHz, so the transmit clock at 2.5MHz; a 160MHz FPGA receiver allows the LTC1864's full 20MHz SCK."
  }
 ],
